
Generally Singleton dp is widely used in java and spring boot

Spring Framework: Default Bean Scope
    This is the "bread and butter" of Spring Boot. In Spring, the default scope for any Bean you create is Singleton.

    The Usage: When you annotate a class with @Service, @Repository, or @Component, Spring creates exactly one instance of that class per application context.

    The Benefit: If 50 different classes need to use your UserService, Spring doesn't create 50 objects.
                 It creates one and "injects" that same instance everywhere. This saves a massive amount of memory.


@Bean
public CacheManager cacheManager() {
    return new ConcurrentMapCacheManager();
}

